# Reaction-mechanism rewrite as (L, K, R) graph data: valence
# preservation across the allyl shift C1=C2-C3 -> C1-C2=C3 (an H
# transfers from C3 to C1), checked by the std.chem package's
# graph_rewrite_preserve cell.
#
# Representation (all generic carriers, no per-reaction code):
# - matrix rows = CONTEXT atoms (the three carbons);
# - matrix columns = the union of atoms in L and R (the carbons plus
#   five hydrogens H1a H1b H3a H3b H3c);
# - entry = bond order (1 single, 2 double; 0 = no bond);
# - L, K, R are the rule triple; K is the context (here identical to L
#   because no atom is deleted);
# - valence of a row = its bond-order sum, the generic matvec(A, 1s);
# - the cell's certificate is
#   sum(abs(matvec(L,u)-matvec(K,u))) + sum(abs(matvec(K,u)-matvec(R,u)))
#   and must be EXACTLY zero: L and R give [4, 3, 4] per carbon, so the
#   allyl shift is valence-conserving. A rule that drops a double bond
#   without compensation refuses typed ValenceImbalance(residual r).
# Run: emath check language/examples/chemistry/molecular-graph-valence.emath
package std

emath field_pack chemistry:
    exports:
        cell balance
        cell mass_balance
        cell graph_rewrite_preserve
    metadata:
        description allyl shift valence preservation via generic rewrite checker
